# Maqsad AI — repository-wide ignore rules.
# Verified at creation: none of these patterns matched a tracked file, so this
# ignores only build output and local artifacts — it untracks nothing.

# ---- Dependencies ----
node_modules/

# ---- TypeScript / build output ----
*.tsbuildinfo
dist/
build/
out/

# ---- Vite ----
.vite/

# ---- Next.js ----
.next/
next-env.d.ts

# ---- Turborepo ----
.turbo/

# ---- Test coverage ----
coverage/

# ---- .NET (CRM plugins, EDP runtime) ----
bin/
obj/

# ---- Local environment (never commit secrets) ----
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.*.example

# ---- OS / editor cruft ----
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Session logs — appended by .claude/hooks (session-end.js), not source.
**/.claude/sessions/log.md
.claude/sessions/log.md

# Plugin strong-name keys.
*.snk

# Exception: the Report Engine key IS committed, deliberately.
#
# It was not, and it was lost — with it went the ability to rebuild or redeploy the plugin at all,
# because the public key token is part of the assembly identity Dataverse registers. Recovering
# cost a full delete-and-re-register of the assembly, its three plugin types, five steps and two
# Custom API bindings, and an outage while reports could not run.
#
# A strong name is an identity, not a secret: Microsoft is explicit that it is not a security
# boundary, and anyone holding the assembly can strip and re-sign it. Escrow in a secret store is
# still the better practice; committing it to this private repository is the trade taken because
# the failure mode above is certain to recur otherwise, and the key protects nothing.
!projects/report-engine/src/Qdb.ReportEngine.CrmPlugin/Qdb.ReportEngine.CrmPlugin.snk

# Scratch space for the ribbon solution export/import round trip.
projects/report-engine/.ribbon-build/
